# SQL cheat sheet (Postgres)

## Order a query is written vs. run
Written:  SELECT … FROM … JOIN … WHERE … GROUP BY … HAVING … ORDER BY … LIMIT
Run:      FROM/JOIN → WHERE → GROUP BY → HAVING → SELECT → ORDER BY → LIMIT
(So WHERE can't use a SELECT alias, but ORDER BY can.)

## Basics
SELECT code, description FROM services WHERE category = 'lab' ORDER BY code;
WHERE x IS NULL        -- never "= NULL"
WHERE code IN ('70450', '80053')
WHERE description ILIKE '%mri%'   -- case-insensitive match
SELECT DISTINCT payer_name FROM charges;

## JOINs
FROM charges c
JOIN services s     ON s.id = c.service_id        -- INNER: only matching rows
JOIN price_files pf ON pf.id = c.price_file_id
JOIN hospitals h    ON h.id = pf.hospital_id
LEFT JOIN: keep every row from the left table, NULLs where there's no match.
"Rows with no match": LEFT JOIN … WHERE right_table.id IS NULL

## Aggregates + GROUP BY
SELECT category, COUNT(*), MIN(x), MAX(x), ROUND(AVG(x), 2)
FROM … GROUP BY category;
- Every non-aggregated SELECT column must be in GROUP BY.
- Aggregates skip NULLs (COUNT(*) counts rows; COUNT(col) counts non-NULLs).
- COUNT(DISTINCT payer_name) counts unique values.
- HAVING filters groups:  HAVING COUNT(*) > 2

## Conditional logic
CASE WHEN discounted_cash IS NULL THEN 'missing'
     WHEN discounted_cash <= 0     THEN 'bad'
     ELSE 'ok' END AS cash_status
COUNT(*) FILTER (WHERE negotiated_dollar < 0)   -- count only matching rows
COALESCE(discounted_cash, 0)                    -- first non-NULL value

## Window functions (compute per row; rows aren't collapsed)
RANK()       OVER (PARTITION BY service_id ORDER BY discounted_cash)
ROW_NUMBER() OVER (PARTITION BY … ORDER BY …)   -- no ties
AVG(x)       OVER (PARTITION BY service_id)     -- group avg next to each row
PARTITION BY = restart per group; ORDER BY = order within the group.
You can't filter a window function in WHERE; wrap it in a CTE or subquery.

## Percentiles
percentile_cont(0.5) WITHIN GROUP (ORDER BY negotiated_dollar)  -- median
percentile_cont(0.9) …   -- 90th percentile

## CTEs (named sub-steps)
WITH ranked AS (
  SELECT …, RANK() OVER (…) AS rnk FROM …
)
SELECT * FROM ranked WHERE rnk = 1;

## Performance
EXPLAIN ANALYZE <query>;
- Seq Scan = reads the whole table; Index Scan / Bitmap Index Scan = uses an index.
- On tiny tables Postgres often picks Seq Scan even with an index; that's normal.

## Gotchas
- Strings use 'single quotes'; "double quotes" are for identifiers.
- NULL compared to anything is NULL (not true or false).
- Integer division: 7/2 = 3; use 7/2.0 or ::numeric.
- Duplicate rows from a JOIN usually mean a one-to-many join; check your keys.

## psql
\dt  tables   \d name  columns   \pset pager off   \x  expanded rows
\i file.sql  run a file   \timing  show query time   \q  quit